🎯 GOAL

Create a hierarchical date slicer (Year → Quarter → Month → Day) synced across both pages:

Filters apply to all visuals (chart + table)

Works with Dataverse’s Created On (or any date field)

Remains synced even when hidden on the drill-through page

🧩 Step-by-Step: Add a Hierarchical Date Slicer
1. Confirm Your Date Field

You’ll typically have a column like:

Created On

Modified On

Closed Date

We’ll use Created On here.

If it’s a text column, first convert it to a date type in Power Query:

Transform Data → select column → Data Type → Date/Time → Close & Apply.

2. Create a Date Table (Best Practice)

Power BI works best with a dedicated Date table for hierarchy and relationships.

Go to Modeling → New Table, and paste this DAX:

DateTable =
ADDCOLUMNS (
    CALENDAR (DATE(2020,1,1), TODAY()),
    "Year", YEAR([Date]),
    "Quarter", "Q" & FORMAT([Date], "Q"),
    "Month", FORMAT([Date], "MMMM"),
    "MonthNumber", MONTH([Date]),
    "Day", DAY([Date])
)


Mark it as a Date table:

Select the DateTable

Go to Table tools → Mark as date table → select [Date] column

Create a relationship:

DateTable[Date] → test_table[Created On]

✅ This allows all your visuals and slicers to filter test_table properly.

3. Add the Date Hierarchy Slicer

On your main summary page:

Insert a Slicer visual

Add DateTable[Year], DateTable[Quarter], DateTable[Month], DateTable[Day]

Click the ellipsis (•••) on the slicer → Hierarchy mode (so it collapses/expands like a tree).

You can use the dropdown style for compactness:

Select the slicer → under Format → Slicer settings → Style → Dropdown

✅ You now have a hierarchy slicer that expands from Year → Quarter → Month → Day.

4. Sync the Date Hierarchy Slicer

Duplicate that slicer on your Drillthrough_Details page:

Copy → paste the slicer from the main page.

Go to View → enable “Sync Slicers” pane.

In the Sync Slicers pane:

For your Date slicer:

Sync: ✅ both pages

Visible: show on main page, hide on drill-through page (optional)

✅ Now, when a user selects 2025 → Q3 → October, both pages filter to those records.

5. Test the Workflow

On the main page, select:

Year = 2025

Month = October

Observe that your clustered chart updates accordingly.

Right-click any bar → Drill through → Details page.

The details table now shows only records for October 2025 — and for that specific Organization + Status.

6. (Optional) Show Active Range

You can display a dynamic date label on your report:

Selected Date Range :=
VAR MinDate = MIN('DateTable'[Date])
VAR MaxDate = MAX('DateTable'[Date])
RETURN
"Showing data from " & FORMAT(MinDate, "MMM d, yyyy") &
" to " & FORMAT(MaxDate, "MMM d, yyyy")


→ Add a Card visual using this measure to show the current filter range.

✅ Final Structure Summary
Page	Visual	Key Filters / Sync
Main Summary	Clustered Column/Bar Chart + Date Hierarchy Slicer	Filters by DateTable, Organization, Status
Drillthrough_Details	Table (with hidden synced slicers)	Syncs with date + other slicers automatically
Supporting Measures	Active Record Count, Drillthrough Title, Selected Date Range	Provide dynamic summaries

  =====================================

  🚀 GOAL

Visualize and compare record counts per Status across different Organizations, using live data from Dataverse (test_table),
showing only active records, with drill-through to view supporting rows.

🧩 Step-by-Step Guide (All-in-One)
1. Connect to Dataverse

In Power BI Desktop → Home → Get Data → Dataverse

Select your environment → choose the table test_table → Load

2. Filter for Active Records

You can filter in Power Query or by creating a DAX measure.
Here we’ll use a DAX measure (flexible and dynamic).

In Report view, select test_table in the Fields pane.

Go to Modeling → New Measure.

Paste this DAX code:

Active Record Count :=
CALCULATE(
    COUNTROWS('test_table'),
    'test_table'[statecode] = 0   // Replace with your “active” flag column if named differently
)


✅ This measure counts only active records (statecode = 0 is the usual “Active” value in Dataverse).

3. Create the Clustered Chart

From the Visualizations pane, choose Clustered Column Chart (or Bar Chart).

Add the following fields:

X-axis: Status

Legend: Organization

Values: Active Record Count (the DAX measure)

Resize and format as you like.

✅ You now have a clustered bar/column chart showing one bar per Organization for each Status — using only active records.

4. Create a Drill-through Page

This page will display the actual records behind any bar.

Add a new page → rename it to Drillthrough_Details.

With the page selected, open the Visualizations pane.

Under the Drill-through filters section, drag:

Status

Organization

(Optional) Add a page filter for active records:

Drag statecode into Filters on this page and select “Active” (or 0).

5. Add a Table of Records

On the same page:

Insert a Table visual.

Add the following fields from test_table:

Record Name (or Title)

Status

Organization

Owner

Created On

Any other useful fields

Resize the table for readability.

✅ This table will automatically filter to show the records for the Status and Organization that were drilled through.

6. Add a Dynamic Title (Optional but Recommended)

Add a dynamic page title that updates based on the drill-through context.

Insert a Text box.

Click inside it → in the formula bar, click fx (expression icon).

Choose Format by: Field value
→ Select Measure (we’ll create one below).

Create this DAX measure:

Drillthrough Title :=
"Details for " & SELECTEDVALUE('test_table'[Organization]) &
" – " & SELECTEDVALUE('test_table'[Status])


Now assign this measure as the title expression.

✅ Your details page title now reads, for example:
“Details for Org A – In Progress”

7. Add a Back Button

Insert → Buttons → Back.

Power BI automatically sets it to return to the previous page (no configuration needed).

8. Test It

Go back to your main chart page.

Right-click on any bar → Drill through → Drillthrough_Details.

You’ll see:

The title updates with the chosen Organization + Status.

The table lists all the active records that make up that bar.

✅ Final Structure Summary
Page	Visual	Purpose	Key Fields / Measures
Main Summary	Clustered Column/Bar Chart	Compare record counts by Organization & Status	Status, Organization, Active Record Count
Drillthrough_Details	Table	Show detailed records supporting each bar	Filters: Status, Organization, (optional) statecode = Active
DAX Measures	Active Record Count, Drillthrough Title	For filtering and display	Dynamic & reusable
